and you know so many businesses were  closed during the pandemic  uh and but the ones that were essential  of course  had to find a way to stay open and do  business safely  and herrera family dental is one of  those businesses  and they were hit pretty hard during the  pandemic  but herrera family dental was actually  founded by a local las vegas  who was born and raised here and she  gave  back to her community and she decided  that she was going to invest  she grew up here her parents speaking  spanish and they had to take care of  their patients  and so many others who were afraid to go  anywhere during the pandemic  dr evelyn herrera worked hard to realize  her dream of being a dentist  her private practice in sunrise manor  was open barely a year when the pandemic  forced her to close  we've always been trained to deal with  infectious diseases but i think  the unknown was more scarier than  anything and she worried for her  patients  their oral health so how long were we  going to not be able to see patients  because  they still need to be able to take care  of the oral health dr herrera was forced  to furlough  all but one staff assistant left to  cover phones and help her with emergency  cases  patients who truly couldn't wait for  covid to be over i was in excruciating  pain my  tooth decided it wanted to hurt it was  march right at the very beginning gloria  long hernandez  has been a loyal patient for five years  seeing dr herrera in various dentist  offices  and now here at herrera family dental on  lamb and charleston  both share roots of growing up in east  las vegas when she started she told me  i want to help my community she says i'm  going to be a dentist not because i want  to you know she wasn't looking to be  wealthy she wasn't looking for all these  other things she was literally looking  to help the community evelyn herrera's  first language is spanish  her hard-working parents immigrated from  mexico and made school a priority for  their kids  she received scholarships for college  then took out loans to attend unlv  dental school  and with support from her husband and  daughter she  sacrificed to create a successful  practice i know some people think that  it's a rough area  but it's only because it's a little bit  older but we do have a lot of culture  in this community it would be nice and  this is one of the reasons why i came  back it would be nice to see more  businesses pop up herrera family dental  is open again with staff and coveted  safety restrictions still in place  i couldn't have felt more comfortable at  a time when everything was very  uncomfortable i think we did a lot for  their patients and i think they  definitely did appreciate it because a  lot of them still come here and dr  herrera hopes it's the beginning of a  very long relationship  with sunrise manor residents i want to  be that dentist that grows up with the  families that  how i saw my own personal dentist on the  east side  well dr herrera has invested a lot to be  there now 80 percent of her patients  speak spanish only and she said a lot of  the patients there  well maybe not a lot but there are still  quite a few patients that are  still afraid to even come back and so  she's going to be very patient with her  patients  and give them time to come back she  wants them to feel comfortable but  she says she's really happy that she and  her one staff member who we heard from  their alina were able to see  the ones that had emergencies true  emergencies  to keep them out of the er when the  hospitals were so crowded with coveted  patients  and so she felt like she was able to do  her part so  dr herrera is a true success story here  in the east valley one of many  eastsiders  that are giving back to their community
 

